  would be a durable log asserting that people authenticated successfully when nothing was ever verified — worse than no
  audit trail, because it looks authoritative.

  [1mPath upgrade[0m

  You chose a persisted store / audit record. This repo has no backend, no storage, no dependencies, no logger beyond
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m, and no test setup. A login-audit store is new structure rather than a change to an existing flow, which
  makes this [1marchitectural[0m, not a bounded edit. The brainstorming ratchet is one-way, so I'm stepping up: clarifying
  questions, then approaches, then a written spec for your review before any code.

  First question — everything else depends on it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "who logged in" is an identity the system already has, or one that has to be established first.
  These lead to completely different amounts of work.

  - [1mIt's the username[0m — the audit record keys on what the user typed. Nothing new to resolve; [38;5;153mlogin[39m already has it.
    Cheap, and you can add a real ID later without redoing the audit shape if we record a stable field name now.
  - [1mIt's a server-assigned ID from a real login response[0m — requires [38;5;153mlogin[39m to actually call [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, handle failure,
    and read an ID back. That's implementing authentication for real, and the audit work sits on top of it. Not cheap,
    and not reversible in the sense that the stub can't come back.
  - [1mIt's a client-side identifier[0m (device/session/anonymous visitor ID, generated locally) — tracks [3msessions[0m, not
    verified people. Cheap and honest about what it is, but it answers "which browser attempted a login", not "who
    logged in".

  My recommendation is the third option only if you want telemetry, and the second if you genuinely want an audit trail
  — because an audit record of an unverified stub isn't an audit trail. If this is a fixture repo and you just want the
  plumbing shape in place, say so and I'll aim for the first with a clearly-labeled stub boundary.

